```# Docker Commands for the Laravel Installations 

## Build Docker Image

The command to build docker image is as follows,

```bash
docker build -t [name_of_your_image]:[tag] [path_to_your_Dockerfile]
```

* `[name_of_your_image]`: the name of the Docker image.
* `[tag]`: the version of the Docker image.
* `[path_to_your_Dockerfile]`: the path to the Dockerfile.

## Run Docker Container

To run docker container, use the following command,

```bash
docker run -p [host_port]:[container_port] -d [imagename]
```

* `[host_port]`: the port number on the host to which the service will be exposed.
* `[container_port]`: the port number on the container on which the service is running.
* `[imagename]`: the name of the Docker image.

## List Active Docker Containers 

The command to list active docker containers is as follows,

```bash
docker ps
```

## Stop Docker Container

The command to stop a docker container, with an active container id, is as follows,

```bash
docker stop [container_id]
```

* `[container_id]`: the id of the container you wish to stop.


# Laravel Installation Example:

## Step 1: Download Laravel installation package 

You can use the below command to get Laravel installation package:

```bash
curl -sS https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
```

## Step 2: Create a new Laravel project 

Create a new Laravel project via artisan:

```bash
php artisan new [ProjectName]
```

* `[ProjectName]`: the name of your new project.

## Step 3: Change Directory to Your Project

```bash
cd [ProjectName]
``` 

* `[ProjectName]`: the name of your new project.

## Step 4: Install Laravel

To install Laravel, you can use the following command:

```bash
composer install
```

## Step 5: Run Your Laravel Application

```bash
php artisan serve
```

This will create a development server that's accessible from your browser via localhost:8000.

**Note:** It's recommended to use a `Dockerfile` for a better dockerized environment. The Dockerfile could look like this,

```Dockerfile
# Use the official Laravel image.
FROM laravel/laravel:latest

# Set the working directory.
WORKDIR /var/www/html

# Copy the current directory contents into the container at /var/www/html.
COPY . /var/www/html

# Install dependencies.
RUN composer install

# Run Laravel
CMD ["php", "artisan", "serve", "--host=0.0.0.0"]
```

Build Image Using This Dockerfile 

```bash
docker build -t my-laravel-app .
```

Run The Application

```bash
docker run -p 8000:8000 -d my-laravel-app
```

Then, you can access your laravel application via `localhost:8000` in the browser. (replace `8000` with the port you want to use) 

To stop Docker container, you can use the `docker stop` command as shown in docker commands. 

Let me know if you need any more help.

**Note:** Be sure that Docker is installed and running in your machine before executing these commands.


