In the class I want PUT_AddContract method to run in the first tex box and PUT_AddContract in different class(i.e., Class1) to run in the second text box. The reason is, it depends on the value of "arrList" and "x". Hence, it needs to be separate. In the first box, it should add the element into an array and in the second box, it should check if the passed element is present in the array.
Please let me know how to achieve this.
The classes and methods should be separate due to these reasons. How can I achieve this?
The code above is not correct because it's trying to add elements to the ArrayList "arrList", which is null at the moment. Therefore, I need to rethink this approach. Are my expectations correct?
Sorry for the confusion. The confusion arises because of the differences in the class creation and the naming conventions. I am confused as to how I need to divide these two tests into different sections. 

Please guide me how I can create two separate test methods for these scenarios. These tests should not interfere with each other and should execute independently.

A: Based on your requirements, you want to have the PUT_AddContract method in two different text boxes with separate functionality. The first text box will add an item to the list, and the second text box will check the presence of an item in the list.

Here's how you can create two separate test methods for these scenarios:

In the first text box, you can create a method with the name PUT_AddContract. Inside this method, you can initialize an instance of ArrayList and add an item to it using the Add method.

In the second text box, you can create a separate method with the name PUT_ContainsContract. Inside this method, you can check if the item exists in the ArrayList. For this, you can use the Contains method of the ArrayList class.

Here's an example implementation:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class ArrayListTest
{
    [TestMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract()
    {
        // Create a new ArrayList instance
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

        // Add an item to the ArrayList
        int x = 5;
        arrList.Add(x);

        // Assert that the item was added to the ArrayList
        Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void PUT_ContainsContract()
    {
        // Create a new ArrayList instance
        ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

        // Add an item to the ArrayList
        int x = 5;
        arrList.Add(x);

        // Assert that the item exists in the ArrayList
        Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
    }
}
```

With this implementation, the PUT_AddContract method will add an item to the ArrayList, and the PUT_ContainsContract method will check if the item exists in the ArrayList. These two methods can be executed independently without interfering with each other.

I hope this helps you achieve the desired functionality. Let me know if you have any further questions or needs assistance.
