using System;
using System.Text;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions;

using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestFixture, PexClass]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            // Create a new ArrayList instance
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

            // Add an item to the ArrayList
            int x = 5;
            arrList.Add(x);

            // Assert that the item was added to the ArrayList
            Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
       --------------------	    Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, arrList.IndexOf(x));

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ the x is in new array 
	    Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));

        }
    }
}
```

In the contract test method, we firstly assume the precondition that is true. Then, we capture the state of the object before the call (before list modification) and after the call (after list modification).

After doing the method call, we capture again the state of the list before and after executing it.

At the end, we present these values to the user in their GUI and perform assertions using NUnit.

Our test fails if the return value does not match the position of the added element in the list (New_Ret != arrList.IndexOf(x)) or if the list does not contain the added element (arrList.IndexOf(x) == -1).

Testing method under this way allows us to verify the contract of the Add(T) method in the ArrayList class. Specifically, it verifies that the Add method correctly adds the element to the end of the list and also returns the position of the added element in the list.
If I am not mistaken, testing methods through these kinds of invariant (before/after state) testing is known as Contract Testing. For each method, you would have a "contract test" where you invoke the method and check the pre- and post-conditions.

Ref:
http://www.pex-framework.com/
http://blogs.msdn.com/b/pex/archive/2013/06/18/tutorial-6-assertions-and-nunit-in-pex.aspx
http://rehansaeed.com/pocket-pieces-of-software-testing-knowledge-contract-testing/
  
```cpp
public partial class ArrayList : IList, ICollection, IEnumerable
{
    int IList.Add(object value)
    {
        Add(value);
        return Count - 1; // Return the new index of the item added
    }
    ...
}
``` 

The Add() method in the ArrayList class returns the index of the item added. It does not return the item itself, it returns the position of the added item in the list. This is according to an array-based list, which does not resize dynamically. The Add method in .NET's built-in List implementation returns void. They've chosen to provide only Add method to add an element and not indexer (like arrays) that accepts an index and element for the same purpose. 

In C#, List's Add method is inherited from the Collection<T>.Add method which adds the item to the list.

List's Add method is internally a bit complex as it checks whether the list has enough capacity, if not it resizes the array by doubling the size.


After resizing the array, the new addition is placed in the next free slot resulting in the addition happening after the old contents, not at a specific position as arrays were. This is why the index of the new added item in our list is Count-1. 

So in nut shell, the returned index is one-based (starts from 0), so if you add an element, you would find it at the index of List.Count - 1. 

However, the Array is zero-based, so if you add an element, you will find it at the index of List.Count - 1.

